jwt — third-party attribution
====================================================================

This file carries a CONDITION, added 2026-09-09. `src/rfc9964_vectors.zig`
reproduces the JOSE examples published in RFC 9964 Appendix A.1 verbatim.
Redistributing this module — alone, or as part of zig-libs — must keep this
file with it.

WHY THIS FILE DID NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW. `README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement
answers a different question — it lists the eleven RFCs this module implements
clean-room — and says nothing about the vectors the module also ships. The gate
written for exactly this shape could not see them: `check-catalog`'s data-file
claim looked for a committed file that is neither Zig source nor documentation,
and these examples live in a `.zig` file, so no witness was found and the check
never ran. That hole was closed on 2026-09-09; this file is one of the four
records it forced.

No code changed, and none needs to. The examples are unaltered.

WHAT WAS TAKEN
--------------

  - `src/rfc9964_vectors.zig` — the ML-DSA JOSE examples of RFC 9964
    Appendix A.1, verbatim. They are self-checking in a way hex KATs are not:
    a transcription error makes ML-DSA verification fail, so the test that
    consumes them is also the test that they were copied correctly.

THE GRANT THIS RESTS ON
-----------------------

Not the merger doctrine. RFC examples of this kind are Code Components in the
sense of the IETF Trust Legal Provisions (BCP 78) — the Trust's list names
"tables of values" explicitly — and TLP 5.0 §4.c grants them under the Revised
BSD License. A written grant is available at no cost, so this module takes it
rather than arguing that published example values are uncopyrightable. That is
the same choice `modules/mls/NOTICE` made on 2026-09-08 and the one
`modules/ctap2pin/NOTICE` already cited.

